        "description": "A voltmeter can show 12.6V on a dying battery due to surface charge. Only a hydrometer measuring electrolyte density reveals true chemical capacity.",
        "code": "1. Fully charge the battery for 12 hours until the inverter charger turns to float mode.\n2. Put on rubber gloves and safety goggles. Unscrew the 6 vent caps.\n3. Insert the hydrometer rubber nozzle into Cell 1. Squeeze and release the bulb to draw electrolyte until the internal float moves freely.\n4. Read the specific gravity at eye level:\n   - 1.260 – 1.280 (Green): 100% Fully charged, healthy cell.\n   - 1.200 – 1.220 (White): 50% Discharged or moderate sulfation.\n   - Below 1.150 (Red): Dead cell, severe sulfation, or shorted plate.\n5. Record readings across all 6 cells. Return acid slowly into the exact same cell.",
